[% if typescript_components %]
---
description: A complete application stack with two enforced ownership boundaries.
---

# TypeScript components architecture

There are two workspaces and one lock: `@exordio/web` and `@exordio/ui`. The
application owns routes, data, credentials, authentication and feature behavior.
The UI package owns presentation, interaction primitives, layouts, semantic
Tailwind tokens and stories. It is consumed directly as TypeScript source;
Next.js transpiles it and Storybook uses Vite. There is no publishing or UI build
pipeline to coordinate.

## Follow the dependency direction

`web` may import `ui`. `ui` cannot import the app, Node built-ins, Next.js,
Better Auth, Drizzle, PostgreSQL, or an undeclared runtime dependency.
`just typescript::boundaries` inspects TypeScript import syntax and rejects those
violations, including dynamic imports and relative paths escaping the package.
Next.js `server-only` markers protect database, email and session code from client
bundles. Database mutations repeat authentication and ownership checks on the
server; a hidden button is never the authorization boundary.

Keep app-local feature modules together. A generic table accepts typed data and
callbacks; its project query and server actions belong to `web`. Do not create
packages for auth, databases, utilities or configuration without a concrete
consumer requiring them.

## Theme and source ownership

The official shadcn Base UI sources are vendored with license/provenance in the
UI package. Their normal CLI configuration remains usable. Upstream registry
changes are explicitly reviewed as source changes; dependency automation alone
does not update copied components.

One shared stylesheet maps semantic CSS variables into Tailwind utilities.
Light/dark variants, charts, feedback, surfaces and focus states use the same
tokens. Both Next.js and Storybook import it and scan UI plus app sources.
Colocated stories document meaningful variants and states. A local font package
keeps builds and screenshots independent of online font services.

## Execution and ownership

Generation emits data only. Setup installs frozen dependencies and creates only
an ignored local environment file. Local service startup and applying committed
migrations are explicit. `db-generate`, `fmt` and `visual-update` author files;
checks only report defects and write ignored results. Shell entry resolves no
credentials and performs no database changes.

Next.js uses Node 24 while Bun manages workspace dependencies. Stable package
identifiers keep a complete lock shared across generated projects. The same UI
package hosts Storybook; it is a development catalog, not a second product.
Neither deployment nor a hosted authentication, database or visual-testing
service is assumed.

## Verification

Unit tests cover validation, environment constraints and mutation authorization.
Storybook uses real Chromium for component interactions and accessibility.
Playwright exercises account/project workflows in a production build and checks
curated Storybook layouts against reviewed Linux x86-64 images. The default
`check` gate composes these commands. Browser/service preparation belongs to
setup, not checks, so prepared projects can be validated without network access.
[% else %]
---
description: Why the TypeScript starter uses Bun workspaces and direct tool commands.
---

# TypeScript workspace

Bun owns runtime execution, workspace dependency installation and tests. A library
and its executable consumer demonstrate the package boundary without a framework.
TypeScript provides strict static checks and Biome provides formatting and lint.

| Concern | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| Dependencies | Committed `bun.lock` |
| Application and library code | Their workspace packages |
| Build output | Ignored `dist/` |
| Cross-language commands | Root justfile |

The starter has no task scheduler: the workspace is small enough for direct
commands. A generated project can add a build graph when its dependencies justify
one. Installation happens in setup; checks execute locally installed tools.

Script commands explicitly use Bun for JavaScript command-line tools rather than
depending on an ambient Node installation. The Linux devenv selects Biome's
static musl executable from the locked optional packages, avoiding an assumption
about a machine-level ELF loader on NixOS.
[% endif %]
